                  <provenance cert="low">
                     <orgName role="fmo" key="org_474">St Albans, Hertfordshire. Benedictine abbey
                        of St Alban</orgName>
                     <p><q>De Studio. Abbatis</q> on flyleaf.</p>
                     <p>Compare the inscription in Oxford, Bodleian Library, MS Laud Misc. 264,
                        etc., but this is of later date (s. xv instead of s. xiv).</p>
                     <q type="pressmark">de studio abbatis</q>
                  </provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected">
                     <orgName role="fmo" key="org_4">Canterbury, Kent, Benedictine cathedral priory
                        of Holy Trinity or Christ Church</orgName>
                     <p>Attributions for both Christ Church and St Augustine's were rejected in the
                        second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964). There were no
                        relevant changes in the Supplement (1987). The corresponding Medieval
                        Libraries of Great Britain index card for Christ Church records the
                        following information, which was deemed unsatisfactory for an attribution:
                           <q>M. R. James says <q>From Canterbury?</q> in the catalogue, and indexes
                           it under Christ Church; but in <title>ALCD</title> assigns it to St
                           Augustine's.</q></p>
                  </provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected">
                     <orgName role="fmo" key="org_122">Canterbury, Kent, Benedictine abbey of St
                        Augustine (originally of St Peter and St Paul)</orgName>
                     <p>Compare the note for the rejected Christ Church attribution.</p>
                  </provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Given to Trinity by <persName role="dnr"
                        >Thomas Nevile</persName>, dean of Canterbury (c. 1548–1615).</provenance>
               </history>
